// THE THREE TIERS DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT THEY WILL ANSWER AT ALL — and the tier
// that DECLINES is the one that gets the answer RIGHT.
//
// ⚠⚠ THE EXPECTATION IN THIS FILE *IS* THE DISAGREEMENT. Nothing below asserts a
// wrong VALUE; every arm asserts a CAPABILITY — "this tier accepts this query and
// produces an answer" or "this tier's answer is correct" — and the capabilities
// asserted do not agree with each other. When the disagreement is closed, THIS
// FILE MUST GO RED AND BE REWRITTEN, header included. Do not "repair" it by
// re-reading an answer off a run. Same idiom and same reason as
// `tests/logos/fail/wql_agg_avg_struct_arg_fail.logos`, which pinned a hole for
// months with its own header saying so and was rewritten when the hole closed.
//
// ⚠ AND THE FIRST TIME THAT INSTRUCTION CAME DUE, IT DID NOT FIRE. The `isize`
// cell closed on 2026-08-05 and this file stayed GREEN, because the static
// refusal it reported was asserted here only in PROSE — the executable pin was
// `tests/logos/fail/wql_agg_isize_arg_fail`, which is what went red and was
// rewritten. A capability narrated but not run is not pinned by the file that
// narrates it. The header is therefore corrected by hand below, and any cell
// this file claims and does not execute should be read the same way.
//
// ⚠ THE SECOND TIME IT CAME DUE — the `u64` `sum`/`min`/`max` cells, 2026-08-06
// — IT STILL DID NOT FIRE FROM HERE, for the identical reason, and that is
// worth stating flatly rather than papering over: the executable pins were again
// in `fail/` (`wql_agg_wide_int_arg_fail`, `deem_incr_static_minmax_u64_fail`),
// those two files went red and were rewritten as `pass/…_e2e`, and this file
// stayed green while its matrix said REFUSES about three cells that had just
// started answering. Blocks G and H below are the correction and the reason the
// pattern should not recur here: the three cells are EXECUTED, at u64's own
// extremes.
//
// ⚠ AND THE SENTENCE THAT FOLLOWED THAT ONE WAS STILL FALSE. It said "so the
// next move of this row takes this file red on an assertion." MEASURED
// 2026-08-06 by breaking the row deliberately, twice, and rebuilding: with the
// min/max seeds reverted to the SIGNED extrema, and with the batch fold's
// overflow predicate reverted to i64's, THIS FILE STAYED GREEN both times.
//   * block H sums to 2^63+15, and two's-complement addition is bit-identical
//     under either predicate — only the CHECK differs, and this file does not
//     reach the check;
//   * block G's min/max operands (5, 2^64-1) sit outside the shadow of the bad
//     seeds, so a seed leak does not reach them either.
// The executable pins are therefore SINGLE-POINT and elsewhere: the seed rests
// on `deem_incr_static_minmax_u64_e2e` alone, the predicate on
// `wql_agg_wide_int_arg_e2e` alone. Widening THIS file to cover them is worth
// doing; claiming it already does was the third time a file in this arc asserted
// something about its own coverage that a control revert refuted.
//
// ── THE MATRIX — STATIC COLUMN RE-MEASURED 2026-08-06 ───────────────────────
// Over a `u64` / `usize` column, per POSITION, per TIER. ⚠ ONLY THE STATIC
// COLUMN WAS RE-MEASURED. The two runtime columns are carried over from
// 85c9433d unchanged and are NOT a fresh measurement; in particular
// `deem_incr_static_minmax_u64_fail` recorded, before it was replaced, that the
// incremental engine's `min`/`max` over `u64` became CORRECT on 2026-08-05
// (`agg_ord_key`/`el_wrap_ord_key`), which if true makes the INCREMENTAL cell
// below stale. That cell's value is `wql_domain_incr_disagreement`'s to state
// and this arc did not run it, so it is left as it was found and flagged rather
// than edited from a sibling's prose.
//
//                        STATIC          DYNAMIC        INCREMENTAL
//   order by             ACCEPTS, right  ACCEPTS, WRONG shape-refused
//   select               ACCEPTS, right  ACCEPTS        shape-refused
//   aggregate avg        ACCEPTS, right  ACCEPTS        ACCEPTS
//   aggregate min/max    ACCEPTS, right  ACCEPTS, WRONG ACCEPTS, WRONG
//   aggregate sum        ACCEPTS, right  ACCEPTS        ACCEPTS
//   arithmetic `+`       REFUSES         ACCEPTS        ACCEPTS †
//
// ⚠ TWO ROWS OF THE STATIC COLUMN MOVED ON 2026-08-06 AND THIS FILE DID NOT GO
// RED, WHICH IS THE SAME MISS AS LAST TIME AND IS RECORDED AS ONE. Both read
// REFUSES and both are now ACCEPTS, right; the batch fold's accumulator carries
// the column's own type (`el::agg_acc_ty`) with u64's seeds and u64's overflow
// predicate. Nothing here executed those cells, so nothing here could fail — the
// rows were corrected by hand, from the change, and blocks G and H were added so
// that the NEXT move of this row is caught by an assertion.
//
// ⚠ AND THE `arithmetic +` ROW DID NOT MOVE WITH THEM, WHICH IS NOW THE SHARPEST
// CELL IN THE TABLE. `sum(s.x)` over a `u64` column is answered while
// `sum(s.x + 1)` over the SAME column silently drops to an i64 accumulator —
// correctly, because `el_add` computes in i64 and `infer_emit_ty` therefore
// reports the arithmetic node's type as the class representative — and
// `select s.x + 1` is refused outright (`check_arith_operand`,
// `wql_arith_wide_int_fail`). The accumulator follows the COLUMN, never the
// arithmetic over it, and the reason is not an oversight: `sum(s.x + 1)` has an
// ARITHMETIC node for its argument, `infer_emit_ty` reports that node's type as
// the class representative because that is what `el_add` returns, and
// `agg_acc_ty("i64")` is `i64`. The distinction is measured across two files —
// block H here for the accepted half, `wql_arith_wide_int_fail` for the refused
// one — because a refusal cannot be a pass test.
//
// † IN AN INCREMENTAL-ELIGIBLE SHAPE, and the distinction is the whole reason
//   the footnote exists rather than a tick. `incremental()` refuses a BARE
//   `select`, a `where`, an `order by` and a `distinct` for EVERY column type
//   including i64 — that is the shape floor, not a value-domain fact, and
//   `wql_domain_incr_disagreement` block 4 pins it on the i64 control so it is
//   not misread as one. So `select s.x + 1` is shape-refused while
//   `sum(s.x + 1)` is ACCEPTED: the arithmetic over a `u64` column is taken,
//   and the refusal above it is about the query's shape. Both cells are
//   measured below.
//
// The wrong VALUES in that table are the comparison seam's and are pinned
// elsewhere — `wql_domain_runtime_order_c`, `wql_domain_runtime_extremes`,
// `wql_value_domain_tiers_measured`, `wql_domain_incr_disagreement`. They are
// deliberately NOT re-asserted here: a value pinned twice is a value that has to
// be rewritten twice, and the arc that closes the seam owns those four files.
// What THIS file pins is the column of the table the seam fix does not touch —
// WHO WILL ANSWER — and it is built to stay GREEN when the values are fixed.
// That is the point: once the runtime tiers are RIGHT, the static refusal stops
// being a protection and becomes a question, and this fixture is the artifact
// that keeps the question visible.
//
// ── THE STATIC AGGREGATE REFUSAL SURVIVED A COMPARISON-SEAM FIX AND DID NOT
// ── SURVIVE AN ACCUMULATOR FIX, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE FINDING ───────────
// The refusal was raised at `wql/codegen.logos::check_agg_operand` (and its
// arithmetic sibling `check_arith_operand`), and NO COMPARISON IS INVOLVED IN
// ITS ANTECEDENT AT ANY POINT, so no change at a comparison site could move it.
// ⚠ THAT WAS A DERIVATION, NOT A MEASUREMENT, when this file was written, and
// the derivation held: the antecedent has been replaced twice since and both
// times the comparison sites were untouched.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THE ANTECEDENT WAS, AND WHAT IT IS. It was `el_host_carries(et,
// "i64")`, whose whole test is `t.bits < 64` — a claim about THE HOST LANGUAGE,
// because `compute_agg_bodies` printed the argument VERBATIM into
// `let __v: i64 = …` / `el_add(acc, …)` and the host widens an integer only from
// a NARROWER width. On 2026-08-05 it became `el_agg_arg_reaches(et, "i64")` — a
// claim about the VALUE — and the emitter began WRITING the cast through
// `emit_norm_operand`; `u64`/`usize` were still refused, because every route
// into an i64 cell REINTERPRETS them.
//
// ⚠ WHAT MOVED ON 2026-08-06 IS THE SECOND ARGUMENT, NOT THE PREDICATE. It is
// `el_agg_arg_reaches(et, <the accumulator this fold actually declares>)`, and
// the fold now declares `el::agg_acc_ty` of the column — `u64` for the
// `EL_ORD_WRAP` row — with u64's seeds (`agg_acc_least`/`agg_acc_greatest`),
// u64's `<`, and u64's `checked_add` (`el_addu`). The refusal was CORRECT GIVEN
// THE ACCUMULATOR IT WAS TOLD ABOUT and it dissolves when told about the right
// one. Nothing was relaxed at an accumulator that did not move: into `i64`, the
// admitted set is byte-for-byte the old one, which is what keeps
// `wql_arith_wide_int_fail` and `wql_udf_wide_int_ret_fail` red.
//
// ── WHAT THE REFUSAL DOES *NOT* COVER, WHICH IS THE FINDING ─────────────────
// It is NOT a class-level over-refusal. `u8`, `u16` and `u32` widen losslessly
// into i64 (`ElTy.fits`, and under the pre-2026-08-05 antecedent `t.bits < 64`
// as well — the two agree on every width below 64), and every aggregate over
// them is accepted by all three tiers — blocks C below measure that, and without
// it "the static tier refuses unsigned aggregates" would be the wrong sentence
// for the right table.
// The op dimension is closed BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by enumeration: `min`,
// `max` and `sum` reach ONE function with ONE antecedent, so per-op variation is
// not expressible; block C measures the type dimension and one `max` arm
// measures the construction claim.
//
// ── THE CELL THAT MADE THIS A DEFECT REPORT AND NOT A LIMITATION — CLOSED ───
//   POSITION, not TYPE, inside ONE clause of ONE tier. Over the SAME `u64`
//   column, in the SAME `aggregate` clause, `avg` was ACCEPTED and its answer
//   CORRECT while `sum`/`min`/`max` were REFUSED — because `avg`'s fold emitter
//   writes `((s.x) as f64)` itself and the others emitted the argument into an
//   i64 cell. Block B measures `avg`, and its numbers prove something further:
//   that `as f64` is an UNSIGNED conversion. 2^64-1 comes back as 2^64 and 2^63
//   as 2^63, not as -1.0 and -2^63. So the static tier ALREADY HAD a correct
//   wide-unsigned conversion at one aggregate position while refusing three
//   others on the ground that it had none. That self-contradiction is what this
//   file existed to keep visible, and it is gone: blocks G and H answer the
//   other three at the same extremes.
//
//   ⚠ BLOCK B IS KEPT VERBATIM AND IS NOW A REGRESSION GUARD RATHER THAN A
//   DEFECT REPORT. `avg`'s exemption from the normalizing cast (`agg_cast`) is
//   exactly what lets it convert u64 UNSIGNED, and the accumulator change
//   deliberately leaves that exemption alone — routing `avg`'s argument through
//   `as i64` first would destroy the very thing block B measures. The three IEEE
//   bit patterns are how that stays measured rather than assumed.
//
// ── THE SECOND SUCH CELL WAS `isize`, AND IT IS CLOSED (2026-08-05) ─────────
// `isize` is 64-bit and SIGNED, so every one of its values survives `as i64`:
// `el_arith_ok("isize")` is true, `s.v + 1` over an isize column compiled, an
// isize sort key was admitted, and both runtime tiers answered `min`/`max` over
// it correctly at i64::MIN and i64::MAX (block F, still asserted below). The
// static tier refused `sum(isize)` anyway, and its own diagnostic said why —
// "the restriction is this position's, not the type's". A refusal with no
// value-domain ground is not a conservative choice, and it needed no ruling
// about the value domain to remove: the seam was made to ask `ElTy.fits` and to
// WRITE the cast, which is what the other four EL operand positions had always
// done. `tests/logos/fail/wql_agg_isize_arg_fail` became
// `tests/logos/pass/wql_agg_isize_arg_e2e`, which asserts the ANSWERS —
// min/max/sum at isize::MIN and isize::MAX, and the Err at the overflow.
//
// ⚠ THAT CLOSURE WAS THE MODEL FOR THE REMAINING CELLS AND ALSO THE WARNING,
// AND BOTH HALVES PROVED RIGHT. It was available because `isize`'s VALUES were
// never in question; the u64/usize cells were not, and lifting them BY THE SAME
// MOVE — widening what reaches an i64 accumulator — would have emitted a wrong
// number rather than a refusal. What the static tier additionally needed is
// exactly what this paragraph predicted: an accumulator typed with the
// ARGUMENT's own type. `rexpr_walk` hardcoded `Vec<i64>` with signed seeds
// i64::MAX/i64::MIN and raw `<`/`>`, while its KEY vectors already carried the
// key's own type — which is why its `order by` was always right and its `max`
// would not have been. The accumulator now does what the key vector does, and
// the seeds, the comparison and the overflow predicate follow the cell.
//
// ── THE REFUSALS THAT REMAIN LIVE IN fail/, BECAUSE A PROGRAM THAT DOES NOT ──
// ── COMPILE CANNOT BE A PASS TEST ───────────────────────────────────────────
//   `+` over `u64`     → tests/logos/fail/wql_arith_wide_int_fail
//   a UDF returning `u64` → tests/logos/fail/wql_udf_wide_int_ret_fail
//   an `f32` aggregate argument → tests/logos/fail/wql_agg_f32_arg_fail
// The two surviving `u64` refusals are positions whose accumulator IS `i64` and
// cannot move: the EL's checked arithmetic tower computes in i64 by definition,
// so the value has to become an i64 there, and it cannot faithfully.
//   `sum` over `u64` USED to head that list. Its answers are now
//   `tests/logos/pass/wql_agg_wide_int_arg_e2e`; `min`/`max` over `u64` are
//   `tests/logos/pass/deem_incr_static_minmax_u64_e2e`.
// Every static ACCEPTANCE claimed in the matrix above is asserted by this file
// simply by compiling: a `deem` whose query the static tier refuses is a compile
// error, so each `pub deem` below is itself half of an assertion and its answer
// is the other half.
//
// ── A CONSTRAINT FOR WHOEVER CLOSES THE *RUNTIME* SEAM ──────────────────────
// (The static seam is closed; this constraint is about the two runtime tiers,
// whose read-back API is what it describes.)
// `QRows` has five getters — get_i64/get_f64/get_bool/get_str/get_node
// (stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos:68-78) — and none of them is unsigned. There is
// therefore NO expression in the dynamic API for the value 2^64-1: a corrected
// `max(u64)` arm has to assert `get_i64(…) == -1i64` and say in prose that -1 is
// the bit pattern of 2^64-1. Fixing the domain properly means fixing the getter
// set too, and that is a second red in the four value files above.
//
// ⚠ THE EXPECTATIONS ARE HAND-DERIVED CONSTANTS. Nothing here asks `el_ty`,
// `RtVal` or any survey artifact what the answer is, and nothing folds a value
// with the arithmetic the engine folds it with — an oracle that shares the
// algorithm with the code under test agrees with itself by construction
// (`feedback_oracle_must_be_independent`). The three `avg` bit patterns are
// IEEE-754 written out by hand: 5.0 = 1.25·2^2 → exponent 1025 = 0x401 →
// 0x4014000000000000; 2^64 → exponent 1023+64 = 0x43F → 0x43F0000000000000;
// 2^63 → exponent 1023+63 = 0x43E → 0x43E0000000000000.
// Blocks G and H likewise: G is an ORDER fact and needs no arithmetic at all
// (u64's order on 0 … 2^64−1 puts 5 below 2^63 below 2^64−1, so max = 2^64−1 =
// 18446744073709551615 and min = 5), and H is one addition written out —
// 5 + 2^63 + 10 = 2^63 + 15 = 9223372036854775808 + 15 = 9223372036854775823,
// which is ABOVE i64::MAX and therefore not a number an i64 accumulator could
// have produced at all.
//
// ⚠ WHY THE RUNTIME AGGREGATE SOURCE IS {5, 2^63, 10} AND NOT THE EXTREMES. The
// capability arms had to survive BOTH outcomes of the then-open decision about
// what `sum` accumulates in (i64 or u64). 5 + 2^63 + 10 is 2^63+15: it overflows
// NEITHER an i64 accumulator (it lands on i64::MIN+15) NOR a u64 one, so an arm
// asserting "this tier answers" cannot flip to Err because the accumulator
// changed. The decision is now taken for the STATIC tier and its extremes are
// asserted in blocks G/H; this source stays as it is because the two RUNTIME
// tiers still carry `sum`'s own latch defect (silently 0 on a u64 overflow,
// spuriously refused on an i64 one), which is not pinned here — it belongs to
// the four value files named above.
//
// EXIT CODES: 10-19 static `u64` order/select; 20-29 static `avg` over `u64`;
// 30-49 the narrow-unsigned controls; 50-69 the runtime tiers over `u64`;
// 70-89 `isize`; 90-99 the static `u64` aggregate cells that closed on
// 2026-08-06 and the arithmetic seam that did not. All ≤ 122, so nothing
// collides with the 8-bit exit ceiling.
//
// exit 0 = the tier capability matrix is exactly as recorded above — the STATIC
//          column as measured on 2026-08-06, the two runtime columns as carried
//          over from 85c9433d — disagreements included.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.lang.num.float;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

pub schema U : code(0x0E68_0000_0000_0001) { x: u64 = 0, g: i64 = 1, id: i64 = 2 }
pub schema P : code(0x0E68_0000_0000_0002) { x: isize = 0, g: i64 = 1, id: i64 = 2 }
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ U, P };

// ── THE STATIC TIER, over the same column types ──────────────────────────────
// Each of these compiling IS an assertion: the static tier admits a `u64` column
// in the ordering, projection and `avg` positions. The three it refuses cannot
// appear in this file at all — see the fail/ fixtures named in the header.
struct S_u64   { pub x: u64,   pub id: i64 }
struct S_u64g  { pub x: u64,   pub g: i64 }
struct S_isize { pub x: isize, pub id: i64 }
struct S_u8    { pub x: u8,    pub g: i64 }
struct S_u16   { pub x: u16,   pub g: i64 }
struct S_u32   { pub x: u32,   pub g: i64 }

pub deem ord_u64(ss: &[S_u64])   { from ss s select s.id order by s.x }
pub deem sel_u64(ss: &[S_u64])   { from ss s select s.x }
pub deem avg_u64(ss: &[S_u64])   { from ss s group by s.id aggregate a = avg(s.x) select a : f64 }
pub deem ord_isize(ss: &[S_isize]) { from ss s select s.id order by s.x }
pub deem sum_u8(ss: &[S_u8])     { from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = sum(s.x) select a : i64 }
pub deem sum_u16(ss: &[S_u16])   { from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = sum(s.x) select a : i64 }
pub deem sum_u32(ss: &[S_u32])   { from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = sum(s.x) select a : i64 }
pub deem max_u32(ss: &[S_u32])   { from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = max(s.x) select a : i64 }
// The three cells that closed on 2026-08-06. `select` carries NO `: i64`
// annotation and cannot: the aggregate's output is a `u64` now, so pinning the
// element type to i64 would be a `Vec<i64>::push(u64)` — a different red with a
// different message, and worth knowing about when an existing query breaks.
pub deem mm_u64(ss: &[S_u64g])   { from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = max(s.x), b = min(s.x) select (key, a, b) }
pub deem sum_u64(ss: &[S_u64g])  { from ss s group by s.g aggregate a = sum(s.x) select a }

fn mku(d: &Writ, xv: u64, gv: i64, i: i64) -> WAny {
    let r: U = d.make::<U>(); r.x = xv; r.g = gv; r.id = i;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}
fn mkp(d: &Writ, xv: isize, gv: i64, i: i64) -> WAny {
    let r: P = d.make::<P>(); r.x = xv; r.g = gv; r.id = i;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    // ══ A. THE STATIC TIER ADMITS A `u64` COLUMN WHERE IT IS COMPARED AND
    //       PROJECTED, AND IS RIGHT AT THE EXTREMES. Codes 10-19.
    //       5 < 2^63 < 2^64-1 is u64's own order, so the ids come back 0, 2, 1.
    let us: [S_u64; 3] = [S_u64 { x: 5u64,                    id: 0i64 },
                          S_u64 { x: 18446744073709551615u64, id: 1i64 },
                          S_u64 { x: 9223372036854775808u64,  id: 2i64 }];
    let o: Vec<i64> = match ord_u64(&us[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } };
    if o.len() != 3i64     { return 11i32; }
    if o.get(0i64) != 0i64 { return 12i32; }   // 5
    if o.get(1i64) != 2i64 { return 13i32; }   // 2^63
    if o.get(2i64) != 1i64 { return 14i32; }   // 2^64-1
    let p: Vec<u64> = match sel_u64(&us[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 15i32; } };
    if p.len() != 3i64                            { return 16i32; }
    if p.get(0i64) != 5u64                        { return 17i32; }
    if p.get(1i64) != 18446744073709551615u64     { return 18i32; }
    if p.get(2i64) != 9223372036854775808u64      { return 19i32; }

    // ══ B. IN THE SAME CLAUSE OF THE SAME TIER, `avg` OVER THAT COLUMN IS
    //       ACCEPTED AND CORRECT WHILE `sum`/`min`/`max` ARE REFUSED. Codes
    //       20-29. One row per call, so the average IS the value and no
    //       group-ordering assumption is made. The bits are IEEE-754 by hand:
    //       an unsigned conversion sends 2^64-1 to 2^64 and 2^63 to 2^63; a
    //       SIGNED one would send them to -1.0 and -2^63, so these three
    //       constants are also the measurement of which conversion it is.
    let b0: [S_u64; 1] = [S_u64 { x: 5u64,                    id: 0i64 }];
    let b1: [S_u64; 1] = [S_u64 { x: 18446744073709551615u64, id: 0i64 }];
    let b2: [S_u64; 1] = [S_u64 { x: 9223372036854775808u64,  id: 0i64 }];
    let a0: Vec<f64> = match avg_u64(&b0[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 20i32; } };
    if a0.len() != 1i64                                   { return 21i32; }
    if f64_to_bits(a0.get(0i64)) != 4617315517961601024u64 { return 22i32; }  // 5.0
    let a1: Vec<f64> = match avg_u64(&b1[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 23i32; } };
    if a1.len() != 1i64                                   { return 24i32; }
    if f64_to_bits(a1.get(0i64)) != 4895412794951729152u64 { return 25i32; }  // 2^64
    let a2: Vec<f64> = match avg_u64(&b2[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 26i32; } };
    if a2.len() != 1i64                                   { return 27i32; }
    if f64_to_bits(a2.get(0i64)) != 4890909195324358656u64 { return 28i32; }  // 2^63

    // ══ C. THE NARROW-UNSIGNED CONTROLS. Codes 30-49. These widen losslessly
    //       into i64 and are accepted in every aggregate position by the static
    //       tier — so the refusal is the 64-bit row, not "unsigned". The u32
    //       `max` arm is the op-dimension control: one function, one antecedent.
    let c8: [S_u8; 3] = [S_u8 { x: 5u8,   g: 0i64 },
                         S_u8 { x: 255u8, g: 0i64 },
                         S_u8 { x: 128u8, g: 0i64 }];
    let s8: Vec<i64> = match sum_u8(&c8[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 30i32; } };
    if s8.len() != 1i64        { return 31i32; }
    if s8.get(0i64) != 388i64  { return 32i32; }          // 5 + 255 + 128

    let c16: [S_u16; 3] = [S_u16 { x: 5u16,     g: 0i64 },
                           S_u16 { x: 65535u16, g: 0i64 },
                           S_u16 { x: 32768u16, g: 0i64 }];
    let s16: Vec<i64> = match sum_u16(&c16[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 33i32; } };
    if s16.len() != 1i64         { return 34i32; }
    if s16.get(0i64) != 98308i64 { return 35i32; }        // 5 + 65535 + 32768

    let c32: [S_u32; 3] = [S_u32 { x: 5u32,          g: 0i64 },
                           S_u32 { x: 4294967295u32, g: 0i64 },
                           S_u32 { x: 2147483648u32, g: 0i64 }];
    let s32: Vec<i64> = match sum_u32(&c32[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 36i32; } };
    if s32.len() != 1i64              { return 37i32; }
    if s32.get(0i64) != 6442450948i64 { return 38i32; }   // 5 + 2^32-1 + 2^31
    let m32: Vec<i64> = match max_u32(&c32[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 39i32; } };
    if m32.len() != 1i64              { return 40i32; }
    if m32.get(0i64) != 4294967295i64 { return 41i32; }   // 2^32-1, unsigned order

    // ══ D/E. THE TWO RUNTIME TIERS ANSWER THE THREE QUESTIONS THE STATIC TIER
    //       REFUSES. Codes 50-69. NO VALUE IS ASSERTED for `u64` here — the
    //       values are the comparison seam's and belong to the four files named
    //       in the header. What is asserted is that the query is accepted and
    //       produces a row, which is exactly the half a seam fix must NOT move.
    let d: Writ = writ_new(32768i64);
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();
    let ua: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    ua.push(mku(&d, 5u64,                   0i64, 0i64));
    ua.push(mku(&d, 9223372036854775808u64, 0i64, 1i64));
    ua.push(mku(&d, 10u64,                  0i64, 2i64));
    let pa: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    pa.push(mkp(&d, 5isize,                    0i64, 0i64));
    pa.push(mkp(&d, -9223372036854775808isize, 0i64, 1i64));
    pa.push(mkp(&d, 9223372036854775807isize,  0i64, 2i64));
    let mut e: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    e.bind_source("us", WAny::ref_to(ua as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    e.bind_source("ps", WAny::ref_to(pa as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));

    let qm: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from us s group by s.g aggregate a = max(s.x), b = min(s.x), t = sum(s.x) select (a, b, t)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 50i32; } };
    let rm: QRows = match qm.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 51i32; } };   // DYNAMIC: answers
    if rm.row_count() != 1i64 { return 52i32; }
    if rm.col_count() != 3i64 { return 53i32; }
    let gm: IncrJoin = match qm.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 54i32; } };   // INCREMENTAL: answers
    if gm.had_error() { return 55i32; }
    let sm: QRows = gm.snapshot();
    if sm.row_count() != 1i64 { return 56i32; }

    // the arithmetic position, which the static tier also refuses
    // (`wql_arith_wide_int_fail`) and both runtime tiers take.
    // ⚠ `+ 1` AND NOT `+ s.x`, and the reason is a real Err rather than a
    // preference: the dynamic tier's `+` is a CHECKED i64 add, so doubling the
    // 2^63 row is i64::MIN + i64::MIN and the query legitimately fails. That is
    // the accumulator's own latch, it is the open D1 axis, and an arm asserting
    // "this tier accepts arithmetic here" must not be decided by it.
    let qa: Query = match Query::compile("from us s select s.x + 1", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 60i32; } };
    match qa.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_r) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 61i32; } }     // DYNAMIC: answers
    // …and the incremental tier refuses THIS ONE for its SHAPE, not for the
    // arithmetic: a bare `select` is refused for every column type including
    // i64 (`wql_domain_incr_disagreement` block 4). The very same arithmetic
    // inside an aggregate — an incremental-eligible shape — is ACCEPTED, which
    // is what makes the row above a value-domain cell and not a shape cell.
    match qa.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_g) => { return 62i32; } Result::Err(_e) => { } }     // INCREMENTAL: shape
    let qz: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from us s group by s.g aggregate a = sum(s.x + 1) select a", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 63i32; } };
    match qz.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_r) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 64i32; } }     // DYNAMIC: answers
    match qz.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_g) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 65i32; } }     // INCREMENTAL: answers
    // `avg` over the same column, both runtime tiers — the position the static
    // tier ALSO accepts, so this is the one aggregate row where all three agree.
    let qv: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from us s group by s.g aggregate a = avg(s.x) select (key, a)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 66i32; } };
    match qv.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_r) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 67i32; } }
    match qv.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_g) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 68i32; } }

    // ══ F. `isize` — THE CELL THAT CLOSED. Codes 70-89. All three tiers agree
    //       here now: both runtime tiers give the CORRECT extremes (and will
    //       keep giving them after any comparison-seam change, because a signed
    //       comparison is already the right comparison for a signed type), and
    //       the static tier ANSWERS `sum`/`min`/`max` over an isize column as of
    //       2026-08-05 — `tests/logos/pass/wql_agg_isize_arg_e2e` holds those
    //       values, and this block deliberately does not repeat them. What stays
    //       here is the ORDER key, which was admitted before the closure and is
    //       the arm that made the refusal legible as the aggregate position's.
    let ps: [S_isize; 3] = [S_isize { x: 5isize,                    id: 0i64 },
                            S_isize { x: -9223372036854775808isize, id: 1i64 },
                            S_isize { x: 9223372036854775807isize,  id: 2i64 }];
    let oi: Vec<i64> = match ord_isize(&ps[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 70i32; } };
    if oi.len() != 3i64     { return 71i32; }
    if oi.get(0i64) != 1i64 { return 72i32; }   // i64::MIN
    if oi.get(1i64) != 0i64 { return 73i32; }   // 5
    if oi.get(2i64) != 2i64 { return 74i32; }   // i64::MAX

    let qi: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ps s group by s.g aggregate a = max(s.x), b = min(s.x) select (a, b)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 75i32; } };
    let ri: QRows = match qi.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 76i32; } };
    if ri.row_count() != 1i64                                   { return 77i32; }
    if ri.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 9223372036854775807i64         { return 78i32; }
    if ri.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != -9223372036854775808i64        { return 79i32; }
    let gi: IncrJoin = match qi.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 80i32; } };
    if gi.had_error() { return 81i32; }
    let si: QRows = gi.snapshot();
    if si.row_count() != 1i64                                   { return 82i32; }
    if si.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 9223372036854775807i64         { return 83i32; }
    if si.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != -9223372036854775808i64        { return 84i32; }

    // ══ G. THE STATIC TIER ANSWERS `min`/`max` OVER `u64`, AT u64's EXTREMES.
    //       Codes 90-94. This row read REFUSES until 2026-08-06 and the refusal
    //       was pinned in fail/, not here — see the second ⚠ at the top.
    //       u64's order is the numeric one on 0 … 2^64−1, so over
    //       {5, 2^63, 2^64−1} the max is 18446744073709551615 and the min is 5.
    //       An i64-seeded fold would answer max = 9223372036854775808
    //       (i64::MIN's bits, a value in no row) and rank 2^63 above 2^64−1.
    let gs: [S_u64g; 3] = [S_u64g { x: 5u64,                    g: 0i64 },
                           S_u64g { x: 9223372036854775808u64,  g: 0i64 },
                           S_u64g { x: 18446744073709551615u64, g: 0i64 }];
    let mm: Vec<(i64, u64, u64)> = match mm_u64(&gs[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 90i32; } };
    if mm.len() != 1i64                              { return 91i32; }
    if mm.get(0i64).1 != 18446744073709551615u64     { return 92i32; }
    if mm.get(0i64).2 != 5u64                        { return 93i32; }

    // ══ H. AND `sum`, UNDER u64's OVERFLOW PREDICATE. Codes 95-99.
    //       {5, 2^63, 10} = 2^63 + 15 = 9223372036854775823 — a value ABOVE
    //       i64::MAX, so it is not merely the right answer, it is an answer the
    //       accumulator this position used to declare could not hold at all.
    //       The two extreme sums (the representable 2^63 and the u64 overflow)
    //       are `wql_agg_wide_int_arg_e2e`'s, not repeated here.
    let hs: [S_u64g; 3] = [S_u64g { x: 5u64,                   g: 0i64 },
                           S_u64g { x: 9223372036854775808u64, g: 0i64 },
                           S_u64g { x: 10u64,                  g: 0i64 }];
    let sv: Vec<u64> = match sum_u64(&hs[..]) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 95i32; } };
    if sv.len() != 1i64                        { return 96i32; }
    if sv.get(0i64) != 9223372036854775823u64  { return 97i32; }

    return 0i32;
}
